# ── API Server ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Point this at your running api-server.
# Local dev (same machine):
EXPO_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://localhost:3000
# If testing on a physical phone (replace with your Mac's local IP):
# EXPO_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://192.168.1.x:3000
# Production (your deployed server URL):
# EXPO_PUBLIC_API_URL=https://api.yourapp.com

# ── Google Sign-In ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Client IDs from Google Cloud Console → APIs & Services → Credentials. These
# are public identifiers, not secrets — they travel in the authorization URL of
# every sign-in — so committing them is fine.
#
# The "Continue with Google" button is hidden on any platform whose ID is unset,
# so leaving these blank disables the feature cleanly rather than showing a
# button that fails after the user has been through Google's consent screen.
#
# Sign in with Apple needs nothing here: it is configured by the capability on
# the App ID and the `usesAppleSignIn` flag in app.json.
# EXPO_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_IOS_CLIENT_ID=
# EXPO_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_ANDROID_CLIENT_ID=
# EXPO_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_WEB_CLIENT_ID=

# ── Legal documents ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# OPTIONAL — an override, not a requirement. The API server already serves both
# documents at <origin>/privacy and <origin>/terms (routes/legalPages.ts), so
# when this is unset constants/legal.ts derives the base from
# EXPO_PUBLIC_API_URL above. That is not a guessed domain: it is the server this
# build is already talking to.
#
# The derivation only fires for a public https origin with no port and no path.
# A dev value — http://localhost:3000, an https LAN address, a .local name — is
# refused, PRIVACY_POLICY_URL and TERMS_URL stay null, the Profile rows say the
# documents aren't published yet, and legalLinksConfigured() reports false. So a
# dev build never claims a link that would 404 during store review.
#
# Set this when the documents should be read somewhere other than the API's own
# host — a marketing domain, say. An explicit value always wins and is used
# verbatim, without the origin check.
#
# Whichever way it resolves, the URL must work before you submit: both stores
# fetch the privacy URL during review, and a 404 — or a page belonging to
# someone else — is a rejection, not a warning.
# EXPO_PUBLIC_LEGAL_BASE_URL=https://4formai.com

# Shown as the Support row in Profile. Null until set, and the row says so
# rather than opening a mail composer addressed to nobody.
EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPPORT_EMAIL=
